    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "df12223d_5c04e480",
        "filename": "core/java/android/net/SocketKeepalive.java",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 189,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-01-21T03:54:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "General concern (food for thought, not necessarily needs some change) : from a software engineering point of view is it fine to have abstract start() but concrete stop() that calls mService.stopKeepalive ? It sounds to me like strong coupling and requiring the subclasses to call some form of starting the keepalive ; at the same time it means the children classes are entitled to have a start() method but no corresponding stop() method which makes them harder to understand. It may make sense as a default implementation, but then it raises the question of whether start() should have a default implementation too.\n\nJust food for thought.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 189,
        "startChar": 25,
        "endLine": 189,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "1cf4d35d8db693812b21afb9559063b99b7249bf",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
